\details{
Create an object defining the parameter values for fitting the models of artificial censoring
(deviation from a strategy). For \code{"initiateWithin"} strategies a binary model at the end of the grace
period is used by default (\code{initiateWithinModel = "binary"}), or the initiation hazard model
(\code{initiateWithinModel = "hazard"}). For \code{"notInitiateWithin"} and \code{"neverInitiate"} strategies the
hazard of initiation is modelled with \code{hazardModelType}.

The default hazard model, pooled logistic regression with a daily indicator time basis, is the
exact discrete-time hazard of initiation. The Cox model treats the initiation days as continuous
times with Breslow ties, so its survival of non-initiation, \verb{exp(-Lambda0(d + 1) exp(X beta))}, is
biased when persons initiate on the same day: \code{exp(-h)} is larger than \code{1 - h} for a daily
probability \code{h}, and the error builds up over the days of the horizon. The bias of the Cox weights
therefore grows with the horizon and with each person's daily probability of initiating; it is
largest for \code{"neverInitiate"} strategies. For long horizons, prefer the pooled logistic model, with
a longer \code{intervalLength} if daily intervals are too costly.

\code{pooledLogisticEngine} chooses how the pooled logistic hazard model is fit. \code{"lr"} (the default)
expands the risk set to one row per person and interval, with the person's covariates, the time
basis and the interactions on every row. \code{"plr"} gives Cyclops's pooled logistic regression
(\code{modelType = "plr"}) the covariates once per person, each person's intervals (as a first and last
interval, or as a list when the indicator basis leaves out an interval), the time basis per interval
and the interactions as covariate-by-basis products, so its memory stays near that of one row per
person. A \code{"day0"} interaction column that is not a basis column enters with its main effect fixed
at 0. With a fixed prior variance (or no prior) the two engines agree to about 1e-7 in the
coefficients and weights. With cross-validation they can choose different variances: the
Cyclops version with plr can pick a much smaller variance than \code{"lr"} on the same data (for
example with \code{fold = 5}, or on a sample of the risk set), and when its data-driven
\code{startingVariance} (the default \code{-1}) is left to it, it starts from a value set by the number of
persons rather than person-intervals. \code{"plr"} also does not flag separation in the interaction
columns, which \code{"lr"} reports as a failed fit. So \code{"lr"} stays the default.

\code{maxCohortSizeForFitting} applies to every censoring model: the binary models at the end of the
grace period sample their starters and non-starters (through \code{\link[CohortMethod:createPs]{CohortMethod::createPs()}}), and a
hazard model whose risk set holds more persons is fit on a uniform sample of that many persons and
then predicts for every person (see \code{\link[=fitCensoringModels]{fitCensoringModels()}}).

\strong{Weights of \code{"notInitiateWithin"} after the grace period.} With \code{postGraceModel = "hazard"} (the
default), the weight of a \code{"notInitiateWithin(G)"} clone after day \code{G} is \code{1 / S(G | X)}, where
\code{S(G | X)} is the probability of not initiating on days \verb{0..G} from the initiation hazard model, the
same model that gives the weights on days up to \code{G}. With \code{postGraceModel = "binary"}, it is
\code{1 / (1 - pi(X))}, where \code{pi(X)} is the probability of starting by day \code{G} from the binary model at
\code{G}, the model the \code{"initiateWithin(G)"} strategy uses (the fit is shared when the analysis has such
a strategy for the same treatment, and made otherwise, with cross-validation when the prior uses
it). The initiation hazard model gives the weights on days up to \code{G}, and all weights of
\code{"neverInitiate"} strategies, under both options.

When each option is valid:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{"binary"}} needs two assumptions. (1) \emph{No effect of grace-period initiation on grace-period
risk.} The binary model is fitted on the persons still under follow-up at \code{G}, so \code{1 - pi(X)} is
the probability of not starting by \code{G} among persons with no outcome or death by \code{G}. It equals
\code{S(G | X)} only when starting the treatment during the grace period does not change the risk of
the outcome or death during the grace period. When starting lowers that risk (a treatment that
acts within days), the persons who started are over-represented among those still under
follow-up at \code{G}: \code{pi(X)} is too large, the weights \code{1 / (1 - pi(X))} are too large in a
covariate-dependent way, and the risks of the \code{"notInitiateWithin"} strategy are biased (in a
simulation in which starting cut the outcome hazard by 80\% from the next day, the risk at day 60
was biased by +0.020, z = 12, coverage 0.72, while \code{"hazard"} was unbiased).
\code{\link[=computeGracePeriodSelection]{computeGracePeriodSelection()}} checks this assumption; \code{\link[=computeCensoringModelCalibration]{computeCensoringModelCalibration()}}
cannot, because the binary model reproduces the observed share among the persons under follow-up
at \code{G} by construction. (2) \emph{A correctly specified binary model:} a logistic regression of
starting by \code{G} on the main effects of the covariates. With continuous covariates that change
\emph{when} persons start, the probability of starting by \code{G} is not logit-linear in them.
\item \strong{\code{"hazard"}} follows each person only until the outcome or death, so it needs neither
assumption (1) nor a model of the selected survivors. It needs a correctly specified hazard model.
Without interactions (\code{covariateTimeInteraction = "none"}) the covariate effects on initiation are
the same on every day of the grace period; when covariates change \emph{when} persons start (for
example who starts on day 0, the day of the index visit), \code{1 - S(G | X)} is then miscalibrated.
With \code{covariateTimeInteraction = "day0"} (the default) the covariate effects on day 0 differ from
those on later days, which handles covariates that change start timing on day 0; \code{"interval"}
lets them differ in every interval of the time basis.
}

Compare the options on your data with \code{\link[=computeCcwDiagnostics]{computeCcwDiagnostics()}} (calibration, the grace-period
selection check, weights, balance and positivity per option) and \code{\link[=computeCcwSensitivity]{computeCcwSensitivity()}} (the
estimates per option).

\strong{Weights of \code{"initiateWithin"} after the grace period.} With \code{initiateWithinModel = "binary"} (the
default), the weight of an \code{"initiateWithin(G)"} clone after day \code{G} is \code{1 / pi(X)}, where \code{pi(X)} is
the probability of starting by day \code{G} from the binary model at \code{G}. With
\code{initiateWithinModel = "hazard"}, it is \code{1 / (1 - S(G | X))}, where \code{S(G | X)} is the probability of
not initiating on days \verb{0..G} from the initiation hazard model through \code{G} (with
\code{hazardModelType}, \code{timeBasis} and \code{covariateTimeInteraction}), and the stabilized weight multiplies
by \code{1 - S-bar(G)} of its numerator model. That hazard model is the one of a \code{"notInitiateWithin(G)"}
strategy of the same treatment (the fit is shared when the analysis has one). When each option is
valid:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{"binary"}} is fitted on the persons under follow-up at \code{G}, whose probability of starting by
\code{G} is the one the weight needs, so it is valid whether or not starting during the grace period
changes the risk during the grace period. It needs a correctly specified binary model: a logistic
regression of starting by \code{G} on the main effects of the covariates. With covariates that change
\emph{when} persons start (for example who starts on day 0), the probability of starting by \code{G} is not
logit-linear in them, and the weights are biased (in simulations with five covariates, and with
covariates that change who starts on day 0, the risk of the \code{"initiateWithin"} strategy at day 60
was biased by -0.26 and +0.32 empirical standard errors).
\item \strong{\code{"hazard"}} models the timing of initiation, with covariate effects that can differ on day 0
(\code{covariateTimeInteraction = "day0"}) or in every interval (\code{"interval"}). But \code{1 - S(G | X)} is
the probability of starting by \code{G} of all persons at day 0, followed until the outcome or death,
not of the persons still under follow-up at \code{G}. The two agree only when starting the treatment
during the grace period does not change the risk of the outcome or death during the grace period:
the same assumption (1) as \code{postGraceModel = "binary"} above, the other way round. When starting
lowers that risk, the persons who started are over-represented among those under follow-up at \code{G},
so \code{1 - S(G | X)} is too small for them and the weights after \code{G} too large. It also needs a
correctly specified hazard model.
}

\code{\link[=computeGracePeriodSelection]{computeGracePeriodSelection()}} checks the assumption of \code{"hazard"}; a flag there means that
\code{"binary"} is the valid choice. \code{\link[=computeCensoringModelCalibration]{computeCensoringModelCalibration()}} compares \code{pi(X)} and
\code{1 - S(G | X)} with the observed share who started by \code{G} (with the same caveat: under grace-period
selection the observed share is that of the selected survivors).

\strong{The \code{"initiateWithin"} regime (\code{graceRegime}).} "Start within the grace period" does not say on
which day of it a person starts, and two regimes are in use (plan section 2.2). They agree up to
\code{G} and differ after it when the start day within the grace period changes later risk.
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{"binaryAtG"}} (the default): the clones still followed after \code{G} (those that started by \code{G})
are weighted by \code{1 / pi(X)}, the inverse probability of starting by \code{G} of the persons under
follow-up at \code{G}. The weight is the same for a start on day 0 and on day \code{G}, so each person still
followed at \code{G} is represented by the starters of the same covariates, pooled over their start
days: after \code{G}, the regime follows the risk of a start on a day drawn from the starters' start
days. This is standard clone-censor-weight practice and gives stable weights.
\item \strong{\code{"naturalGrace"}} (Cain et al. 2010; Wanis et al. 2024): persons who would start during the grace
period start on their own day, and persons still untreated on day \code{G} start on day \code{G}. Its
weights are the last-interval weights: 1 for the clones that started before day \code{G}, and
\verb{1 / P(start on day G | not started before G, X)} for those that start on day \code{G}. With
\code{initiateWithinModel = "binary"} that probability comes from a binary model at \code{G} fit by
\code{\link[CohortMethod:createPs]{CohortMethod::createPs()}} on the persons who reach day \code{G} untreated and under follow-up, with the
response "started on day \code{G}"; with \code{"hazard"} it is the initiation hazard of day \code{G},
\code{1 - S(G | X) / S(G - 1 | X)}, whose risk set on day \code{G} is the same persons (so the grace-period
selection that biases \code{initiateWithinModel = "hazard"} under \code{"binaryAtG"} does not arise). With
the pooled logistic hazard model the last interval must be day \code{G} alone (\code{graceEnd} a multiple of
\code{intervalLength}). The weights of few clones (those that start on day \code{G}) vary, so they are less
stable than those of \code{"binaryAtG"}. Only those clones stand for the persons who reach day \code{G}
untreated, so the regime needs starts on day \code{G}: \code{\link[=fitCensoringModels]{fitCensoringModels()}} warns when nobody starts
on day \code{G}, and \code{\link[=computePositivity]{computePositivity()}} shows covariate strata where few do. When few day-\code{G}
starters carry the weight after \code{G}, the standard errors of risks at horizons shortly after \code{G}
fall with the estimate and their intervals can under-cover (simulation of plan section 9 item 7,
\code{G = 7}, 6000 persons with about 140 day-7 starters and 14 of their outcomes by day 14: coverage
0.89 to 0.90 at day 14, 0.95 to 0.96 at day 60; with 24000 persons, 0.94 to 0.95 at day 14).
The stabilized weights after \code{G} multiply by the same quantity for every clone (the marginal
probability of starting by \code{G} of the persons under follow-up at \code{G}), so the weighted
Kaplan-Meier risks do not depend on stabilization.
}

\code{\link[=computeTrueRisks]{computeTrueRisks()}} gives the truth of either regime (its argument \code{graceRegime}). The choice is a
choice of estimand, not of model: \code{\link[=computeCcwSensitivity]{computeCcwSensitivity()}} reports both.

\strong{\code{"initiateWithin"} strategies with \code{graceStart = S > 0}.} A start before day \code{S} is a deviation,
so the weights of days up to \code{S} are those of a \code{"notInitiateWithin(S - 1)"} strategy, \code{1 / S(d - 1 | X)} from the initiation hazard model through \code{S - 1} (with \code{initiateWithinModel = "binary"}; the
fit is shared with such a strategy in the analysis) or through \code{G} (with \code{"hazard"}). The binary model
at \code{G} (and the hazard model's probability, \code{1 - S(G | X) / S(S - 1 | X)}) is then that of starting
on days \code{S..G} of the persons who have not started before day \code{S}, and multiplies \code{S(S - 1 | X)}
after \code{G}. With the pooled logistic hazard model and \code{initiateWithinModel = "hazard"}, \code{S} must be a
multiple of \code{intervalLength}.

\strong{Covariate-by-time interactions.} With \code{covariateTimeInteraction = "day0"} (the default), the
pooled logistic initiation hazard model also has, for each covariate, its product with an indicator
of the first interval of the time basis. With daily intervals (\code{timeBasis} with
\code{intervalLength = 1}, the default) that is day 0, so the covariates can change initiation on day 0
differently from later days. With longer intervals the model has one row per person and interval,
so it cannot tell day 0 from the other days of the first interval: \code{"day0"} is then the product
with the indicator of the first interval (days \verb{0..intervalLength - 1}). Use daily intervals when
initiation on day 0 itself depends on the covariates differently. With \code{"interval"}, the model has
each covariate's product with each time-basis column (with the indicator basis: with the indicator
of each interval except the reference), so the covariate effects can differ in every interval.
With \code{"none"} it has no interactions. The interaction columns are penalized by the prior like the
covariates; the time-basis columns are never penalized. The Cox model has no interactions, so with
\code{hazardModelType = "cox"} the default is \code{"none"}, and \code{"day0"} and \code{"interval"} are not available.

Settings saved before \code{covariateTimeInteraction}, \code{postGraceModel}, \code{initiateWithinModel} and
\code{graceRegime} existed load with \code{"day0"} (\code{"none"} for the Cox model), \code{"hazard"}, \code{"binary"} and
\code{"binaryAtG"}.
}
